STEP 25 — If a container exits

Never immediately recreate everything.

First:

docker ps -a

Find:

Exited (1)

Then:

docker logs <container-name>

For example:

docker logs inventory-service

If you see:

ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:5432

check its source:

grep -n -E "DATABASE|DB_|postgres|5432|process.env" \
services/inventory-service/src/index.js

Find the expected environment variable.

Then recreate that one container.

STEP 26 — Important container recreation rule

If a container already exists:

docker run ...

will give:

Conflict. The container name is already in use.

Do:

docker rm -f <container-name>

Then run the docker run command again.

For example:

docker rm -f inventory-service

then:

docker run ...
STEP 27 — Important: docker ps vs docker ps -a

Remember:

docker ps

shows running containers only.

docker ps -a

shows:

running
stopped
exited

So if you don't see a service in:

docker ps

check:

docker ps -a
STEP 28 — Daily startup after everything is created

Once all 8 containers have been successfully created, you don't need to use all the long docker run commands every time.

Start PostgreSQL:

sudo systemctl start postgresql

Start Docker services:

docker start catalog-service
docker start inventory-service
docker start notification-service
docker start auth-service
docker start order-service
docker start payment-service
docker start analytics-service
docker start frontend

Then:

docker ps
STEP 29 — Daily shutdown

Stop application containers:

docker stop \
  catalog-service \
  inventory-service \
  notification-service \
  auth-service \
  order-service \
  payment-service \
  analytics-service \
  frontend

You don't necessarily need to stop PostgreSQL unless you specifically want to:

sudo systemctl stop postgresql
